</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.1.1. Quantitative Results ‣ 6.1. Error Identification Study ‣ 6. Evaluation ‣ DPCheatSheet: Using Worked and Erroneous LLM-usage Examples to Scaffold Differential Privacy Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the regression results for precision. Among the four independent variables, only DPCheatSheet intervention had a significantly positive effect on precision scores (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.659" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.659</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.659</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Since the other variables did not show significant effects, we conducted a follow-up comparison of precision scores between the two instructional conditions (DPCheatSheet vs. baseline). As precisions were not normally distributed (Shapiro–Wilk <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>), we applied a Mann–Whitney U test. The result showed a significant difference in precision scores between the two groups (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.000</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that DPCheatSheet led to higher precision scores than the baseline method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 6.1.1. Quantitative Results ‣ 6.1. Error Identification Study ‣ 6. Evaluation ‣ DPCheatSheet: Using Worked and Erroneous LLM-usage Examples to Scaffold Differential Privacy Implementation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the regression results for recall. Again, only DPCheatSheet intervention had a significant effect (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.514" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.514</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.514</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) on recall, indicating that DPCheatSheet helped students identify more actual mistakes compared to the baseline. As recall scores were non-normally distributed (Shapiro–Wilk <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>), a Mann–Whitney U test shows a significant group difference (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.000</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12590v1/identified_mistakes.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/151;" width="310" height="151"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S6.F4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Number of participants in each group who correctly identified each mistake. Participants in the DPCheatSheet group consistently outperformed the baseline group, with more than half identifying <span id="S6.F4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">misused sensitivity</span>, <span id="S6.F4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unprivatized data-dependent hyperparameters</span>, <span id="S6.F4.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only partially privatized</span>, and <span id="S6.F4.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overly noisy result</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among the five underlying issues, participants receiving DPCheatSheet performed best at identifying <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partially privatized logic</span>, with 11 participants (92%) catching it. <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Misuse of sensitivity</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unprivatized data-dependent hyperparameters</span> were each identified by 10 participants (83%). Six participants (50%) correctly spotted <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overly noisy results</span>, and 3 participants (25%) caught<span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exceeded privacy budgets</span>. In comparison, fewer participants in the baseline group identified each of the five mistakes. The most commonly identified error in the baseline group was <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unprivatized data-dependent hyperparameters</span>, with 4 participants (33%) recognizing it. None of the baseline participants noticed <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overly noisy results</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>. </span><span id="S6.T7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Participants’ responses to the NASA TLX survey, reported as median (mean <math id="S6.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation) on a 7-point Likert scale. Arrows indicate the desired direction for each measure (<math id="S6.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that a higher value is better, and <math id="S6.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that a lower value is better). For each measure, we highlighted the value with the better median in bold.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S6.T7.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T7.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T7.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T7.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S6.T7.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S6.T7.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S6.T7.5.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
